      en: "Sultan Abdul Kahar's tomb (r. 1430–1440) commemorates a ruler who expanded Brunei's territory to include parts of the Philippines. The structure is a traditional Malay house-style with a pitched roof, built from ironwood and bamboo. It was restored in 1960 after a storm damaged the roof. The cenotaph inside is covered with a yellow cloth symbolizing royalty. A 2012 dendrochronology study dated the timber to 1435. The site is located near the Brunei River and is protected by a flood barrier built in 1970. It is less frequented due to its secluded location. The tomb is noted for its simple design compared to later sultans' mausoleums. The caretaker lives on site and offers guided explanations.",
