      en: "Sultan Abdul Jalilul Akbar's tomb (r. 1620–1640) marks a period of territorial expansion. The two-storey mausoleum, built in 1972, features a green dome symbolizing Islam. The lower level houses relatives' graves. The mahogany cenotaph was carved by local craftsmen. The sultan was ambushed and killed in 1640 during a campaign in Sabah. An adjacent ancient monument from 1760 is a stele with Jawi script. The site includes a waiting hall for visitors. A 2013 renovation added solar-powered lighting. Annual visitors average 1,500. The caretaker gives historical talks.",
